WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.485 --> 00:00:07.860
SPEAKER 1: So
we're going to talk

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.720
about Cloud-Native Computing.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.220
What's Cloud-Native Computing?

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.082
It's all of these words.

00:00:13.082 --> 00:00:15.540
Let's highlight some of the
ones that are most interesting.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.140
So Cloud-Native Computing
is an approach to computing

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.920
that prioritizes things
like containerization,

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.360
immutable infrastructure,
declarative APIs,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.660
loose coupling.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.140
And this allows you
to have principles

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.723
like observability, automation,
and really good predictability.

00:00:32.723 --> 00:00:34.140
These are the
characteristics that

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.682
are embedded in
modern infrastructure

00:00:35.682 --> 00:00:38.045
and are particularly
exemplified by Kubernetes.

00:00:38.045 --> 00:00:39.420
Who here has heard
of Kubernetes?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.896
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:40.896 --> 00:00:41.880
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.350
Good.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.870
Thanks for being awake for at
least part of the last couple

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.320
days.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:47.820
All right.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.390
So modern infrastructure
has been about the evolution

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:57.080
from treating servers like pets
to treating them like cattle.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.330
And that's the benefit of
using a cloud infrastructure

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.850
like Google Compute Engine.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.932
But cattle are still solid,
substantial, long-lived things.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.640
You can say, look at
that cow over there,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.720
that one by the fence.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.300
You know, that one with the
sarcastic look in her eyes.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.517
But with the next
wave of computing,

00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:15.350
things like infrastructure,
you can't really

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.700
grab hold of servers
like cattle are.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.710
The nodes, the containers, the
addresses, the pods, they all

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.420
blink in and out of existence.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.450
And they're really only
comprehensible as a collective.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.420
It's less like cattle, and
it's more like a big frothing

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.820
mass of microorganisms.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.370
But you know what you get
from a big frothy mass

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.920
of microorganisms?

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.910
Beer.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.558
So modern
infrastructure is great.

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:42.600
But it's very different
from the traditional ways

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:43.890
of doing computing.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.290
So now, let's take a look at
how we've been doing CI/CD.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.120
Here are some of the
models that describe

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.170
a typical CI/CD systems.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.160
So first, we have the
Rube Goldberg machine.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.860
You push a button here, and
that schedules a job there,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.803
and that opens a
socket over there.

00:01:57.803 --> 00:01:59.220
And that sends an
XML message over

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.807
to that thing, which writes
generated code onto a floppy.

00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:03.390
And you give it to
the intern, and she

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.090
runs it across the street
and puts it in the mainframe,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:07.590
catapults it up onto prod.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.340
And you know what?

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.960
It works.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.480
But these things are kind of
like a Heisenberg property.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.930
If you peel back and try
and look at it, oh, no.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:15.430
Never mind.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:15.930
Never mind.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.740
Walking away.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.810
So it's very fragile.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.345
Here is, of course,
the Frankenstein model.

00:02:22.345 --> 00:02:23.470
It's been cobbled together.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:24.428
And it's one of a kind.

00:02:24.428 --> 00:02:26.640
Beautiful, really, in its way.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.030
And this guy, he's going to
lurch and lumber, slow perhaps,

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.210
but steady to get your
stuff from source to prod.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.880
And everyone once in a while,
one of his arms will fall off,

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.220
but if you know where to
find Dr. Frankenstein,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.700
you can call him up and he'll
stitch that arm back on.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.670
And of course, the
model of the hairball.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:48.390
So we use these terms to
describe our CI/CD systems,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.610
and we use them
affectionately, because we

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.220
crafted them and we
know all their quirks.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.410
But let's face it,
these systems are pets.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.357
In fact, this is exactly
what my cat looks like.

00:02:58.357 --> 00:03:00.690
When I talk to customers about
the setups they're doing,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.120
I often hear things like
we have one big server

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.430
and only Susan is
allowed to touch it.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.380
Or we upgraded our
system, and now we

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.470
can run two simultaneous tests.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.450
Or we have one
staging environment,

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.000
and the DBAs are
always messing it up.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.540
This approach, it has worked,
sort of, for a long time.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.140
But now that we moved to a
new kind of infrastructure,

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.070
we need to change
how we do CI/CD.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.100
We need Cloud-Native CI/CD.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.230
So right now, we
as an industry are

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.790
at the beginning
of a major effort

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.380
to establish a
new kind of CI/CD.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.380
This is an effort to
modernize software delivery

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.713
to take advantage of all the
benefits of cloud computing

00:03:38.713 --> 00:03:40.280
so that we can get
things like speed,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.565
scalability, portability,
and improved quality.

00:03:44.565 --> 00:03:46.940
Last month, the Linux Foundation
announced the Continuous

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.610
Delivery Foundation, or CDF.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.860
This is a sister project to
the Cloud-Native Computing

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.240
Foundation.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.270
The goal of the CDF is to
evangelize CI/CD, define

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.070
best practices, and
support collaboration

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.300
between practitioners
and vendors.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.795
These are the premier
members of the CDF,

00:04:03.795 --> 00:04:05.170
and they include,
as you can see,

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.920
big tech companies,
CI/CD veterans,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.240
and some customers who are
active and proactive in CI/CD

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.070
like Netflix.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.320
There's also a lot
of vendors that

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.870
are part of the
general member space,

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.390
and a lot of well-known CI/CD
tools are in that space.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:25.630
So a lot of the industry
is coming together here

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.150
to define a next generation.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.940
So the CDF's approach is
heavily informed by Kubernetes.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.050
Kubernetes has
unlocked a new level

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:39.800
of runtime security and
portability and scalability.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.350
So with the CDF, we're
bringing those things to CI/CD.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.940
And we're embedding
best practices

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.850
so that we get containerization,
immutable infrastructure,

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:53.200
declarative APIs, all
that good modern stuff.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.710
The initial projects in the
CDF are Jenkins, Jenkins X,

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.640
Spinnaker, and Tekton.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.170
CDF fosters communication
between these projects

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.480
so that we get interoperability,
and we establish

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.980
shared best practices.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.360
Let's take a closer look
at Jenkins X. Jenkins X

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.640
bundles together several
best-of-breed open source

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.820
technologies, things like
Jenkins, Helm, Skaffold, Prow.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.400
These are all really
cool open source projects

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.140
that do specific things.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.790
Jenkins X puts
them together into

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.490
a pre-wired
batteries-included solution

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.530
based on modern best practices.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.650
And it provides an on
ramp to Cloud Native CI/CD

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.180
while maintaining
a lot of that user

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.620
experience you're familiar
with from Jenkins.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:38.100
So let's see it in action.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.180
Carlos?

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.465
CARLOS SANCHEZ: OK.

00:05:40.465 --> 00:05:44.950
So I have here running
an application.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.460
You can see I have--

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:51.610
this is a very interesting
game, which is shooting lasers

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:53.380
to crocodiles, right?

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.790
I could be playing this forever.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.105
It's a lot of fun.

00:05:58.105 --> 00:06:02.290
And I have it running here
in the staging environment.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:04.050
And I have the
same application--

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.530
let's refresh it--

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.020
in production.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.010
And you can see
here at the bottom

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.580
the version and the
region and the commit

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.570
that is being used to
build these applications.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.810
So this is 134 in production.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.270
I have 134 also in staging.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:28.570
So let's say, what is my
